# ── Line endings ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `* text=auto` was already here and is doing its job: `git ls-files --eol`
# reports i/lf for every tracked file, so the repository is uniformly LF even
# though the Windows working copy is CRLF. Left exactly as it was.
#
# It matters more here than in a normal repo because ONE PHYSICAL WORKING TREE
# is served to two operating systems — Windows (Claude Code, editors) and
# Colab/Linux (the torch steps) — through the Google Drive mount. Without
# normalisation a commit made from Colab could flip a file to CRLF and the next
# diff would show every line changed, burying the real edit.
#
# eol= is deliberately NOT set: that would rewrite the working copy on next
# checkout, and the current setup is already correct. Do not "fix" this.
*       text=auto
*.py    text diff=python
*.md    text
*.ipynb text
*.csv   text
*.json  text
*.txt   text

# ── Shell scripts: LF in the working copy too ─────────────────────────────
# Added 2026-09-14 with the repo's first tracked *.sh (the GROBID WSL manager).
# The note above says eol= is deliberately unset — that is about the global `*`
# rule and it still stands. This rule is narrower, and it is required: the
# script is executed by bash INSIDE WSL but read from the WINDOWS working copy
# at /mnt/d/..., so a CRLF checkout makes the shebang `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`
# and the interpreter is not found. text=auto alone would do exactly that.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# ── Binaries: never diff, never newline-convert ───────────────────────────
# Extended 2026-08-18. Most of these are already ignored ("data that must never
# sneak in"), but `git add -f` bypasses .gitignore, and newline conversion on a
# forced add would SILENTLY CORRUPT a raster, a checkpoint or a GeoPackage.
# Declaring them means that mistake cannot destroy data.
*.xlsx    binary
*.tif     binary
*.tiff    binary
*.pt      binary
*.pth     binary
*.parquet binary
*.gpkg    binary
*.pdf     binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gdoc    binary

# ── Recorded HTTP bodies used as test fixtures ────────────────────────────
# Added 2026-09-21 with the first one (qc/fixtures/litkb_web_snapshot_*.html,
# the Crossref blog article `hunt`'s page route is tested against). These files
# are the BYTES a server sent on a named date, and the tests that read them
# assert a sha256 over exactly those bytes. `* text=auto` would newline-convert
# them on checkout, so the fixture would hash differently on a tree checked out
# on Windows from one checked out on Linux — which is a fixture that is not a
# record. `binary` is the narrowest rule that stops it; the file is still
# perfectly readable text, it is simply never rewritten.
Scripts/qc/fixtures/*.html  binary

# ── Extracted first-page text used as binding fixtures ────────────────────
# Added 2026-09-21 with qc/testdata/litkb_binding_stamps/ (the arXiv margin-stamp
# case, register E25). These files are what `pdftotext -f 1 -l 1 -layout` printed
# for a named PDF, and the binding code reads them LINE BY LINE — a checkout that
# turned them into CRLF would change the bytes a window is scored on, so a fixture
# recorded on Windows and one on Linux would not be the same fixture. `text eol=lf`
# is the narrowest rule that pins it and still diffs; the note at the top of this
# file about eol= is about the global `*` rule, and `*.sh` below already sets the
# precedent for a narrower one.
Scripts/qc/testdata/litkb_binding_stamps/*.txt  text eol=lf

# ── Recorded HTTP cassettes (litkb S4.5 item 7) ───────────────────────────
# Added 2026-09-23 with qc/fixtures/litkb_cassettes/ (litkb/cassette.py). An index is JSONL whose
# every line is a request key and the response a host sent — status, headers, and small bodies inline
# as base64 — and the replay and the manifest identify it by the sha256 of its BYTES. `*.json text`
# (and `* text=auto`) would newline-convert it on checkout, so one recording would hash two ways on
# two operating systems: a fixture that is not a record. The existing `Scripts/qc/fixtures/*.html`
# rule matches the top level only, so the cassette tree needs its own; `**` covers every recording
# and every CONSTRUCTED one beneath it.
Scripts/qc/fixtures/litkb_cassettes/**  binary

# ── Recorded landing pages (litkb S4.5 builder-C2b, Stage C) ──────────────
# Added 2026-09-23 with qc/fixtures/litkb_landing_pages/: one publisher landing page per rule of
# pipeline/litkb/acquire/landing_rules.json, recorded hop by hop (recording.json + hopN.body), every
# body pinned by the sha256 of its BYTES. Same reason as the cassettes: a checkout must not rewrite them.
Scripts/qc/fixtures/litkb_landing_pages/**  binary

# ── A recorded OAI-PMH page (litkb S4.5 builder-fix4, the EarthArXiv harvester) ──
# Added 2026-09-24 with qc/fixtures/litkb_eartharxiv_oai_page7_37129fe75ccb.xml: the BYTES eartharxiv.org served for
# page 7 of its ListRecords feed, pinned by sha256 in qc/test_litkb_s45_prerun.py. Same reason as the .html rule above:
